, "ship-gate", "site-architecture", "skill-security-auditor", "skill-tester", "snowflake-development", "soc2-compliance", "social-media-analyzer", "social-media-manager", "spec-driven-workflow", "spec-to-repo", "spinning-up-deep-rl", "sql-database-assistant", "stage-launch", "stock-analysis", "strategic-alignment", "stress-test", "strict-api", "stripe-integration-expert", "swedish-mentor", "tdd-guide", "tech-debt-tracker", "testrail", "threat-detection", "ui-design-system", "ux-researcher-designer", "video-content-strategist", "vpe-advisor", "vpe-review", "webinar-marketing", "weekly-review", "workflow-builder", "wrap-up", "write-a-skill", "youtube-full", "zero-hallucination-coder", "zz-sql-review-10187", "design", "design-sync", "dataviz", "update-config", "verify", "debug", "code-review", "simplify", "batch", "fewer-permission-prompts", "doctor", "loop", "schedule", "claude-api", "workflow-authoring", "run", "run-skill-generator", "plugin-authoring"], "tests_pass": false, "base": "743c411cd9a74d42ca29812712bddef890fe1bf3", "capture": {"requests": 2, "leaks": [], "system_hashes": ["bf95ca34c568"], "tool_sets": ["ff763d4b64c6"], "request_models": ["claude-sonnet-5-5"], "ratelimit": {"anthropic-ratelimit-unified-overage-status": "rejected", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-representative-claim": "seven_day", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-reset": "1794103200", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-7d-reset": "1794103200", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-7d-status": "allowed", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-fallback-percentage": "0.5", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-overage-disabled-reason": "org_level_disabled", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-7d-utilization": "0.17", "anthropic-ratelimit-unified-status": "allowed"}, "probe_hit": true}}, "trace": {"commands": [], "calls": [["Skill", "zz-sql-review-10187"], ["Read", "/private/tmp/wk/c822a61406/workbench/query.sql"]], "final_message": "**Query:** `SELECT * FROM orders o JOIN users u ON o.user_id = u.id WHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';`\n\nI only have the query text. There's no schema, index list, or `EXPLAIN` output, so the scans below are inferred from the SQL. Check them against a real plan.\n\n## Scans the query forces\n\n1. **Full scan of `users` (non-sargable predicate).**\n   - `lower(u.email)` wraps the column in a function. A plain index on `users.email` can't be used, so the planner has to evaluate `lower()` on every row.\n   - This is the main cost, and it grows linearly with the size of `users`.\n\n2. **Possible full scan of `orders`, or a hash join over it.**\n   - The join uses `o.user_id = u.id`. If `orders.user_id` has no index, the planner can't do an index lookup into `orders` for the one matching user.\n   - It falls back to scanning `orders`, usually with a hash join. That's wasteful when the filter matches roughly one user.\n   - Foreign keys don't create an index on the referencing column in Postgres or SQL Server, though MySQL/InnoDB does.\n\n3. **Wide row fetch from `SELECT *`.**\n   - This pulls every column from both tables, including `u.id` and `o.user_id`, which are duplicated.\n   - It rules out index-only scans.\n   - It also drags wide or TOAST columns through the join.\n   - Selecting `*` from a join can also break callers when columns are added or when names collide.\n\n## Proposed fixes\n\n**1. Index the expression (keeps the query as written):**\n```sql\n-- PostgreSQL\nCREATE INDEX idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n```\nUse a unique index if emails must be unique case-insensitively. That also enforces the rule.\n\n**2. Or avoid the function call.**\n- Use a case-insensitive type or collation (`citext` in Postgres, or a `_ci` collation in MySQL).\n- Or store emails normalized to lowercase and compare `u.email = 'a@example.org'` against a plain index. Normalize on write, and add a `CHECK (email = lower(email))` constraint.\n\n**3. Index the join key:**\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\nIf you usually read recent orders, make it `(user_id, created_at DESC)` instead. That also supports an `ORDER BY` or `LIMIT`.\n\n**4. Select only the columns you need:**\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.status, o.total, o.created_at, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM orders o\nJOIN users u ON u.id = o.user_id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\nIf the column list is covered by an index, you can add `INCLUDE (...)` columns for an index-only scan.\n\n**5. If the caller wants one user's orders, resolve the user first.**\n- The planner usually does this on its own once the indexes exist.\n- If a user can have thousands of orders, add `ORDER BY o.created_at DESC LIMIT n` so the result is bounded.\n\n## Expected result\nWith fixes 1 and 3, the plan should become an index seek on `users` (one row), then an index range scan on `orders.user_id`. Cost then depends on the number of matching orders, not on table size.\n\nTo confirm, run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after. In the \"before\" plan, look for `Seq Scan on users` with a `Filter: (lower(email) = ...)` line.\n\nIf you tell me the database engine and share the schema or an `EXPLAIN` output, I can make these recommendations specific.", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}